    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d564">

                <pb facs="0647" n="637" sameAs="frus:frus1943China/pg637"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">893.00 Tibet/68</note>
                <head>
                    <hi rendition="#italic">The British Embassy to the
                            Department of State
                    </hi><anchor n="25" xml:id="d564-f1"/>
                </head>
                <dateline>
                    <date ana="#date_undated-inferred-from-document-content" calendar="gregorian"
                        when="1943-08-28">August 28, 1943</date>
                </dateline>
                <p rendition="#center">
                    <hi rendition="#small-caps">Paraphrase of Telegram From the Foreign Office to
                        the British Embassy, Washington</hi>, (<hi rendition="#small-caps"
                        >August</hi> 28, 1943)</p>
                <p>The Government of India have received information from reliable sources in Tibet
                    stating that the number of Chinese troops in the Jyekmago [<hi
                        rendition="#italic">?Jyekundo</hi>]<anchor n="26" xml:id="d564-f2"/> area is
                    considerably greater than the garrisons of former years and that additional
                    troops are being recruited and additional arms are arriving. The Tibetan
                    Government are said to have found it necessary to increase their own forces and
                    there is considerable nervous tension.</p>
                <p>The Government of India are instructing their representatives at Lhassa to inform
                    the Tibetan Foreign Office that they have taken note of this information, that
                    they advise them to verify it and to ensure that their own troops do nothing to
                    provoke a frontier incident.</p>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
    <standOff>
        <listAnnotation>
            <note corresp="#d564-f1" n="25" type="source" xml:id="d564-f1-content">
                <p>Handed to the Adviser on Political Relations (Hornbeck) on September 14 by Sir
                    George Sansom of the British Embassy who stated that the British Foreign Office
                    “wish to know whether we would think it possible to have our representative in
                    Chungking express interest in this matter.”</p>
            </note>
            <note corresp="#d564-f2" n="26" xml:id="d564-f2-content">
                <p>Brackets appear in the original.</p>
            </note>
        </listAnnotation>
    </standOff>
